L000303	\subsection{Periodic coupling via the reaction coordinate mapping} \label{rc_mapping}
L000304	
L000305	The approach presented above assumes that the time-dependence is in the system Hamiltonian $H_\mathrm{sys}$ only. Here we describe a method to construct a Floquet-IF for cases where instead the coupling is time-dependent, by using the reaction coordinate (RC) mapping \cite{Hughes2009Jul,chin2010Exact,iles2014Environmental,strasberg2018Fermionic,restrepo2018From,nazir2019The,shubrook2025Non}. In the RC mapping the bath modes are transformed linearly to a new set of modes, consisting of the reaction coordinate $a_0$ and the residual bath ($a_\lambda$ modes). The transformation is designed in such a way that the system couples only to the RC mode
L000306	\begin{equation}
L000307	    H_\mathrm{int}(t)=S(t)\sum_\lambda g_\lambda (b_\lambda+b_\lambda^\dagger)=S(t)g_0(a_0+a_0^\dagger).
L000308	\end{equation}
L000309	The bath Hamiltonian then includes a hopping between the RC mode and the residual bath modes
L000310	\begin{equation}
L000311	    H_\mathrm{env}=\omega_0 a_0^\dagger a_0+\sum_\lambda \tilde g_\lambda(a_0 a_\lambda^\dagger+ a_0^\dagger a_\lambda ) + \sum_\lambda \tilde\omega_\lambda a_\lambda^\dagger a_\lambda.
L000312	\end{equation}
L000313	This allows us to include the RC mode into the system, while treating the residual bath as another stationary environment for which a semi-group IF can be constructed. The reaction coordinate can then be absorbed back into the bath IF, similar to the procedure in Ref.~\cite{Cygorek2021Jan}, recovering a periodic Floquet-IF for the original system.
